<worksheet xml:id="WP-12">
    <title>Week 12 Weekly Practice</title>
   <page>

    <p>
        Due the first class of the week of November 23.
    </p>

    <exercise workspace="2in">
      <title>Exercise 7.2</title>
      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>
             Prove that if <m>a</m> is rational and <m>ab</m> is irrational, then <m>b</m> is irrational.
          </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
          <p>
            We will use a proof by contradiction. So assume the negation which means there exist real numbers 
            <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> such that <m>a</m> is rational and <m>ab</m> is irrational and <m>b</m> is rational.
            Since <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> are rational, there exist integers <m>x,y,z,</m> and <m>w</m> with <m>y,w\neq 0</m>
            such that <m>a = \frac{x}{y} </m> and <m>b = \frac{z}{w}</m>. Then
            <md>
             <mrow>  ab \amp = \frac{x}{y} \cdot \frac{z}{w}</mrow> 
             <mrow> \amp = \frac{xz}{yw} .</mrow>
            </md>
            Since integers are closed under multiplication, <m>xz, yw \in \mathbb{Z}</m>. Since <m>y,w\neq 0</m>, we 
            know <m>yw \neq 0</m>. So, by the definition of rational, <m>ab</m> is rational, which contradicts our assumption
            that <m>ab</m> is irrational. Therefore, the product of a rational and an irrational number is always rational.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>

      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Give an example of two irrational numbers whose product is rational.
          </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
          <p>
            There are lots of examples, but one simple one is choosing both irrational numbers to be <m>\sqrt{2}</m>
            (which we have previously proved is irrational). Then <m>\sqrt{2}\sqrt{2} = (\sqrt{2})^2 = 2</m> and <m>2</m>
            is rational. Said differently, the irrational numbers are not closed under multiplication.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>
      
    </exercise>
   
  </page>

  <page>
    <exercise workspace="1in">
      <title>Exercise 7.3</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Prove that there do not exist integers <m>m</m> and <m>n</m> where <m>12m+21n=1</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p> 
            Suppose for the sake of a contradiction that there do exist integers <m>m</m> and <m>n</m> such that
            <m>12m+21n = 1</m>.  Then
            <md>
              3(4m + 7n)=1.
            </md>
            Since <m>m</m> and <m>n</m> are integers and the integers are closed under addition and multiplication, 
            <m>4m+7n\in\mathbb{Z}</m>. So by the definition of divides, <m>3\mid 1</m>, but that's absurd. So the negation
            is false and  there do not exist integers <m>m</m> and <m>n</m> where <m>12m+21n=1</m>.
          </p>
        </solution>

    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="1in">
      <title>Exercise 7.7</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Let <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> be subsets of some universal set <m>U</m>. 
            Show <m>A\backslash B</m> and <m>B\backslash A</m> are disjoint.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            Recall that two sets are disjoint if they have no elements in common, i.e., the intersection is 
            empty. Suppose for the sake of a contradiction <m>A\backslash B</m> and <m>B\backslash A</m> are not disjoint.
            Then <m>(A\backslash B ) \cap (B\backslash A) \neq \emptyset</m>. In that case there is some element <m>x\in U</m>
            such that 
            <md>
              x\in (A\backslash B ) \cap (B\backslash A).
            </md>
            This means that <m>x \in A\backslash B  </m> and <m>x \in B\backslash A</m>. By definition of set 
            subtraction <m>x\in A\backslash B</m> means that <m>x\in A</m> and <m>x\notin B</m>. On the other hand 
             <m>x \in B\backslash A</m> means that <m>x\in B</m> and <m>x\not in A</m>. But <m>x \in A</m> and <m>x\notin A</m>
             is a contradiction. So the negation is false and we conclude that <m>A\backslash B</m> and <m>B\backslash A</m> are disjoint.
          </p>
        </solution>

    </exercise>
  </page>
    <page>

    <exercise workspace="1in">
      <title>Exercise 7.12</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Are there infinitely many composite numbers? Prove your answer.
          </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
          <p>
            There are indeed. One proof is that there are infinitely many positive even integers, and every positive
            even integer besides <m>2</m> is composite. Alternatively, following along with the infinitely many primes proof
            that uses contradiction, suppose there are finitely many composite numbers, call them <m>c_1,c_2,\dots, c_n</m> for 
            some <m>n\in\mathbb{N}</m>. Then multiply them all and don't add 1: <m>c_1c_2\cdots c_n</m> is a composite number
            that is not in the list.
          </p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

<exercise workspace="1in">
  <title>Exercise 7.15</title>
  <statement>
    <p>
Prove that if <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> are positive real numbers, 
then <md>\frac{x+y}{2} &gt; \sqrt{xy}</md>.<fn>  This is called the “AM-GM” inequality for “arithmetic mean” and “geometric mean”. 
  Notice the left side is how you would take the average or mean of two numbers. 
  The right side is the geometric mean. More info at: 
  <url href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AM%E2%80%93GM_inequality" visual = "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AM%E2%80%93GM_inequality"/> 
  There's a proof there, but you should write your own proof using contradiction.  </fn>
    </p>
  </statement>

  <solution>
    <p>
      We will use a proof by contradiction. Suppose <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> are positive real numbers, and 
      <md>
        \frac{x+y}{2} \leq \sqrt{xy}.
      </md>
      Then, using algebra,
      <md>
        <mrow> \frac{x+y}{2}\amp \leq \sqrt{xy}</mrow>
        <mrow> \frac{(x+y)^2}{2^2} \amp \leq xy </mrow>
        <mrow> x^2 + 2xy+y^2 \amp \leq 4xy  </mrow>
        <mrow> x^2-2xy +y^2 \amp \leq 0 </mrow>
        <mrow> (x-y)^2 \amp \leq 0 </mrow>
      </md>
      This is absurd! The square of a real number is always positive!
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise>

</page>

</worksheet>
